        topics: ["<b style='color:var(--pc)'>\ud83d\udccb You code fast \u2014 Python syntax is 1 day max. Spend the rest on vectorization patterns and getting comfortable with financial data. Add Polars to your toolbelt this week: it's listed alongside pandas in hedge fund job specs.</b>", "<b style='color:var(--pc)'>\u2500\u2500 Python Setup & Your First Stock Chart \u2500\u2500</b>", "Install everything first \u2014 open your terminal and run: <code>pip install numpy pandas matplotlib seaborn yfinance jupyterlab scipy statsmodels</code>. Then open JupyterLab (type <code>jupyter lab</code>) and run <code>import numpy</code> \u2014 if no error, you're set.", "<b>What is NumPy?</b> It's the math backbone of Python. A normal Python list is slow for numbers. NumPy gives you an 'ndarray' (n-dimensional array) that does math 50\u00d7 faster. Think of it like Excel columns \u2014 you apply a formula to the entire column at once, not cell by cell.", "<b>Why no for-loops?</b> Writing <code>for x in arr: total += x</code> is slow. Writing <code>np.sum(arr)</code> is 50\u00d7 faster because it runs in compiled C under the hood. This is called 'vectorization' \u2014 the #1 rule of scientific Python. If you write a for-loop over a NumPy array, you're doing it wrong.", "<b>What is pandas?</b> NumPy is for raw numbers. pandas is for tables with labels (like a spreadsheet in code). The main object is a DataFrame \u2014 rows are dates, columns are stocks. Key operations you'll use daily: <code>.rolling(20).mean()</code> (sliding average), <code>.shift(1)</code> (yesterday's value), <code>.pct_change()</code> (daily % change)."],
