        title: "1B \u00b7 Linear Algebra, Calculus & Probability",
        topics: ["<b style='color:var(--pc)'>\ud83d\udccb The hardest math week. w.T @ \u03a3 @ w (portfolio variance) and Monte Carlo simulation are the two things to truly understand \u2014 they appear in every subsequent phase. Watch 3Blue1Brown before touching any code.</b>", "<b style='color:var(--pc)'>\u2500\u2500 Linear Algebra I \u2014 The Math Behind Portfolios \u2500\u2500</b>", "<b>Vectors and dot products:</b> A portfolio is a list of weights, e.g. w = [0.3, 0.4, 0.3] (30% in stock A, 40% in B, 30% in C). The portfolio's daily return = w \u00b7 r = (0.3\u00d7r_A) + (0.4\u00d7r_B) + (0.3\u00d7r_C). In NumPy: <code>w @ r</code>. The <code>@</code> operator means 'dot product / matrix multiply'.", "<b>Matrices:</b> A matrix is a 2D table of numbers. You can multiply matrix A by matrix B only if A's number of columns equals B's number of rows. Always check with <code>A.shape</code> while learning. The result shape is (A_rows \u00d7 B_cols).", "<b>The central formula of portfolio theory \u2014 \u03c3\u00b2_p = w.T @ \u03a3 @ w:</b> Portfolio variance (risk\u00b2) equals weights transposed \u00d7 covariance matrix \u00d7 weights. Here \u03a3 (Greek letter sigma) is the 'covariance matrix' \u2014 a table showing how much every stock moves with every other stock. This formula underlies ALL of portfolio theory.", "<b>What is a covariance matrix?</b> A square table where entry [i,j] = how much stock i and stock j move together. Values near +1 = they move in tandem (both tech stocks). Values near 0 = independent. Values near \u22121 = they move opposite (gold vs equities). The diagonal = each stock's own variance."],
