        topics: ["<b style='color:var(--pc)'>\ud83d\udccb std::expected, std::mdspan, std::flat_map are the C++23 features trading firms actually use. Templates and Concepts let you write generic, type-safe code with zero runtime overhead \u2014 critical for pricing engines.</b>", "<b style='color:var(--pc)'>\u2500\u2500 C++23 Key Features \u2500\u2500</b>", "std::expected<T,E> (C++23): return either a value OR an error \u2014 like Rust's Result<T,E>. No exceptions in hot path. auto result = pricer.price(params); if (result) use *result; else handle result.error(). Use this for ALL operations that can fail.", "std::mdspan (C++23): multi-dimensional VIEW into contiguous data. No copy, no ownership. Perfect for option pricing grids and matrices: std::mdspan<double, std::extents<int,N,N>> vol_surface(data.data(), N, N); vol_surface[i,j] = 0.25;", "std::flat_map / std::flat_set (C++23): sorted containers backed by contiguous vector. 2\u20133\u00d7 faster iteration vs std::map (which uses a tree). Use for: order book price levels, symbol lookup tables where iteration dominates insertion.", "Smart pointers: unique_ptr<T> (sole ownership, zero overhead), shared_ptr<T> (shared, reference counted), weak_ptr<T> (non-owning observer). Rule: unique_ptr by default, shared_ptr when genuinely shared, never raw pointers for ownership."],
