        topics: ["<b style='color:var(--pc)'>\ud83d\udccb This week permanently changes how you think about code. Every optimisation you did in PHP was high-level (query plans, caching, batching). C++ performance goes down to individual CPU instructions.</b>", "<b style='color:var(--pc)'>\u2500\u2500 C++23 Performance I \u2014 CPU & Cache \u2500\u2500</b>", "Memory hierarchy (memorize these): L1 cache ~4ns, L2 ~12ns, L3 ~40ns, DRAM ~100ns. A cache miss (L3\u2192DRAM) costs 25\u00d7 more than an L1 hit. If your hot loop touches non-contiguous data, you are paying this penalty every iteration.", "Structure-of-Arrays (SoA) vs Array-of-Structures (AoS): for batch processing all prices, SoA (prices[], strikes[], vols[]) is cache-friendly. AoS (struct Option{price,strike,vol}[]) causes cache misses when iterating a single field.", "False sharing: two threads write to variables that share the same 64-byte cache line \u2192 they invalidate each other's cache on every write. Fix: pad structs to 64 bytes using alignas(64). Critical for lock-free queues.", "Branch prediction: predictable branches (always true, alternating) cost ~1 cycle. Mispredicted branches cost ~15 cycles. Use [[likely]] / [[unlikely]] attributes (C++20) to hint the compiler. Profile with perf stat."],
        project: "Cache-Friendly Matrix Multiply Benchmark + SIMD Batch Option Pricer",
