        title: "1L \u00b7 C++23 Concurrency & Lock-Free Data Structures",
        topics: ["<b style='color:var(--pc)'>\ud83d\udccb You've used Redis queues, job queues, async processing in Laravel/NestJS. C++ concurrency is the same concepts without the framework doing the hard parts. std::jthread, std::atomic, SPSC ring buffers.</b>", "<b style='color:var(--pc)'>\u2500\u2500 C++23 Concurrency \u2500\u2500</b>", "std::jthread (C++20/23): like std::thread but auto-joins on destruction (no forgotten joins, no std::terminate). Supports stop_token for cooperative cancellation. Always prefer jthread over thread for new code.", "std::mutex + std::scoped_lock: protect shared data with scoped_lock(mutex) (releases automatically at end of scope). std::scoped_lock<M1,M2> for multiple mutexes (avoids deadlock via lock ordering). Use RAII for all mutex locking.", "std::atomic<T>: lock-free operations on simple types. atomic.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed) for non-critical counters. atomic.compare_exchange_weak(expected, desired) for CAS. Memory ordering: use seq_cst while learning, optimize later.", "Thread pool pattern: create N threads at startup, tasks pushed to a shared queue (mutex-protected std::queue). Threads block on a condition variable until a task is available. std::future + std::promise for return values."],
