        topics: ["<b style='color:var(--pc)'>\ud83d\udccb You've used WebSockets (NestJS), Redis pub/sub, HTTP APIs. C++ networking is lower level \u2014 raw POSIX sockets, UDP multicast, epoll. The concepts map but the code is more explicit.</b>", "<b style='color:var(--pc)'>\u2500\u2500 Network Programming in C++ \u2500\u2500</b>", "POSIX sockets: socket(AF_INET, SOCK_DGRAM, 0) for UDP. bind() to a port to receive. sendto(fd, buf, len, 0, addr, addrlen) to send. recvfrom(fd, buf, len, 0, addr, &addrlen) to receive. Every network program starts here.", "UDP vs TCP for market data: UDP = no connection, no retransmission, ~10\u201350\u00b5s latency. TCP = reliable, ordered, ~100\u00b5s\u20131ms. Exchanges multicast market data via UDP. Order entry uses TCP (need reliability).", "Non-blocking I/O: fcntl(fd, F_SETFL, O_NONBLOCK). With epoll: epoll_create1(0), epoll_ctl(ep, EPOLL_CTL_ADD, fd, &event), epoll_wait(ep, events, MAX, timeout). One thread handles thousands of connections.", "Binary message protocol: define __attribute__((packed)) struct MarketData { uint8_t type; int64_t timestamp_ns; uint32_t instrument_id; double bid; double ask; }. Use htonl/ntohl for byte order. Fixed-size = zero parse overhead."],
