        title: "Project 2 \u00b7 Build Your Own Memory Allocator",
        topics: ["<b style='color:var(--pc)'>\ud83d\udccb Builds directly on Phase 1 week 1K (cache performance) and 1L (concurrency). The pool allocator from this project integrates into Project 1.</b>", "<b>What you're building:</b> Three production allocators in C++23: (1) Arena allocator (bulk allocate, bulk free \u2014 for per-tick allocations), (2) Pool allocator (fixed-size objects, O(1) \u2014 for Order objects), (3) NUMA-aware allocator (pins memory to the correct CPU socket). Benchmark all three vs malloc.", "<b>Why this matters:</b> malloc() can take 500ns-5\u00b5s (sometimes calls the kernel). A pool allocator takes 10-50ns. The difference directly translates to latency. Every HFT firm uses custom allocators.", "<b>Arena allocator:</b> Pre-allocate a large slab (64MB). allocate(N) = advance pointer by N bytes (1-2ns). reset() = move pointer to start (0ns). No individual free \u2014 reset everything at once (per-tick cycle).", "<b>Pool allocator:</b> Pre-allocate N objects of size T. Free list = intrusive linked list through free slots. allocate() = pop from list (O(1), ~10ns). deallocate() = push back (O(1), ~5ns).", "<b>Integration:</b> Replace all new/delete in Project 1's order book with the pool allocator for Order objects. Measure the end-to-end latency improvement."],
        project: "Build Your Own Trading Memory Allocator",
        resources: ["\ud83c\udf10 sourceware.org/glibc/wiki/MallocInternals \u2014 understand what you're replacing", "\ud83c\udf10 github.com/microsoft/mimalloc \u2014 Microsoft's fast allocator, study the source", "\ud83d\udcfa CppCon 2015: 'allocator is to allocation what vector is to vexation' (YouTube)", "\ud83c\udf10 github.com/codecrafters-io/build-your-own-x \u2014 'Build your own Memory Allocator' section"],
        gc: [],
        goals: ["Demonstrate that allocate() and deallocate() are both under 20ns p99", "Show that the reused slot address matches the original allocation (memory reuse confirmed)", "Measure a p99 improvement in the order book after replacing new/delete with the pool allocator", "Publish 'Why Trading Systems Don't Use malloc' as your first technical blog post"],
